2026-07-23 10:46:15,875 INFO 976c31fb-9a6c-4048-b82c-1103ca9359ae: rag_recursive R2 disputed-step query="The inference that Dr. Genius's valuation service constitutes a 'mandate' (Auftrag) under Swiss law, thereby granting Katrina the right to revoke at any time. The plurality answer relies on classifying the engagement as a mandate (Art. 394 ff. OR), which indeed allows for arbitrary revocation with compensation for damages. However, professional services like real estate valuations are typically classified as independent contracts for work and services (Werkvertrag, Art. 394 OR) or general service contracts (Dienstvertrag, Art. 394 OR depending on interpretation of result vs. effort). Under a Werkvertrag, the client cannot arbitrarily revoke the contract at any time without cause; they can only terminate for good cause or by mutual agreement, unless specific statutory provisions for service contracts apply. Even if viewed as a Dienstvertrag, the right to arbitrary termination is not absolute in all professional contexts without considering the nature of the obligation. More critically, statement iii claims every contract falls under one of the types in the second division. The second division of the Swiss Code of Obligations covers 'Contracts'. It is a fundamental principle of Swiss law that unnamed contracts (unnamed contracts) exist and are governed by general principles, meaning statement iii is false. While the plurality correctly identifies iii as false, its justification for i and ii rests on the specific legal characterization of the relationship as a mandate. If the relationship is a Werkvertrag (common for specific deliverables like a valuation report), statement i is legally incorrect because arbitrary revocation is not a standard right. Therefore, the classification step is the weak link leading to the selection of both i and ii. B: Under Swiss law, a contract for professional services like a valuation is often treated as a mandate (Auftrag), which allows for revocation at any time (statement i). However, if this revocation occurs at an inopportune time, the revoking party is liable to compensate the other party for resulting damages (statement ii). Statement iii is incorrect as not all contracts fit neatly into the specific named categories of the Code of Obligations. B: Based on Swiss Code of Obligations principles applicable to service/mandate contracts, a principal may revoke a mandate at any time (i) but must compensate for resulting damages if the revocation occurs at an inopportune time (ii). Statement iii is false as unnamed contracts exist outside specific statutory divisions." retrieved 3 passage(s): ['Gold clause', 'NEC Engineering and Construction Contract', 'Complex Projects Contract']
